{
  "assessment_mode": "sub-agent",
  "assessment_mode_intentional": true,
  "partner_verdicts": [
    {
      "partner": "visionary",
      "verdict": "more_diligence",
      "rationale": "The macro tailwind is real and the team credentials are intriguing, but I need to hear the 10-year platform vision before I can get behind this at a $16M pre. The portfolio conflict with ServiceBot and TechDispatch softens if Cadence can demonstrate a genuinely differentiated wedge — embedded payments, AI dispatch, or a vertical-specific data moat. More diligence on the vision and on conflict resolution."
    },
    {
      "partner": "operator",
      "verdict": "more_diligence",
      "rationale": "The early unit economics are strong — 4.5-month payback at $1,200 CAC is capital-efficient for SMB SaaS. But I cannot underwrite the GTM story without understanding how 120 customers were acquired. No commercial hire, no channel attribution, no churn data. The portfolio conflict is a governance problem that needs legal resolution. Conditional more_diligence: come back with acquisition breakdown, cohort data, and a conflict-resolution path."
    },
    {
      "partner": "analyst",
      "verdict": "hard_pass",
      "rationale": "ServiceBot and TechDispatch are blocking conflicts per our own criteria — this is not a close call. Even setting aside the conflict, the valuation at ~41.7x ARR with only four months of growth data and no NDR/GRR is aggressive. The missing data (burn rate, churn, cohort curves) prevents any credible underwriting. Hard pass unless fund counsel clears the conflict and founders provide a full data room."
    }
  ],
  "debate_sections": [
    {
      "topic": "Portfolio Conflict — Blocking or Waivable?",
      "exchanges": [
        {
          "partner": "analyst",
          "position": "I want to lead with this because it's dispositive. We own ServiceBot — Field Service Management, direct overlap. We own TechDispatch — HVAC/Plumbing Scheduling SaaS, which is exactly the buyer Cadence sells into. Our own evaluation criteria treats a direct blocking conflict as a dealbreaker. I don't see how we get to a term sheet without a formal legal opinion and consent from both portfolio companies. We'd be funding a direct competitor to two founders who trusted us. That's a reputational risk that should weigh heavily here."
        },
        {
          "partner": "visionary",
          "position": "I hear you on the formal conflict question, but let's not short-circuit the analysis. Portfolio conflicts exist on a spectrum. ServiceBot — do we even know what their current product scope is? If they've pivoted up-market into enterprise or focused on a specific vertical like HVAC, then Cadence targeting the broader SMB scheduling market might not be a true overlap in practice. Before we wave the hard-pass flag, I want a real answer to whether these are actually competing for the same customers right now."
        },
        {
          "partner": "operator",
          "position": "I'm somewhere in the middle. The conflict is real, but I've seen funds navigate this before when the market segment is large enough and the companies have differentiated wedges. The question is whether Cadence is going after a meaningfully different buyer or product surface. TechDispatch is vertical-specific — HVAC/plumbing. Cadence, as described, is horizontal across all SMB field service. That might actually be a different enough entry point to clear the conflict with founder consent. But I agree — we can't talk deal terms until we have a legal opinion and conversations with ServiceBot and TechDispatch leadership."
        },
        {
          "partner": "analyst",
          "position": "I'll grant that the practical overlap needs investigation, but let's be honest about incentives here. If we go to ServiceBot and say 'can we invest in your competitor,' what's the realistic answer? Even if they say yes to avoid friction, we've introduced a dynamic where we're receiving confidential information from both sides of the same market. That's not just an LP governance issue — it's a partner reputation issue. I'd rather pass with integrity than close with legal uncertainty hanging over us."
        },
        {
          "partner": "visionary",
          "position": "Agreed on the process point. Let's not pretend we can fast-track around this. But I don't want to let the conflict discussion crowd out a real view on the deal quality. If the conflict were resolved tomorrow — clean legal opinion, both portfolio companies give consent — would we be excited about Cadence on its own merits? That question is worth answering separately."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "topic": "Traction Quality — Is 18% MoM Real and Repeatable?",
      "exchanges": [
        {
          "partner": "visionary",
          "position": "Let me make the bull case on traction. 18% MoM sustained for four months is not noise. That is compounding at roughly 2.3x per quarter. At 120 paying customers and $40K MRR, this is a company that has found something real. The SMB field-service market has historically been badly served by clunky legacy software — ServiceTitan is $20K+ per year, way too expensive for the small plumber or HVAC shop. There is a genuine Shopify-of-field-service opportunity here, and the early velocity is consistent with that kind of wedge."
        },
        {
          "partner": "operator",
          "position": "I want to believe the bull case, but I need to know how those 120 customers were acquired before I can call this a real growth engine. If it was a ProductHunt launch or a viral post that brought in a cohort of one-time sign-ups, the 18% rate is a lagging average of a spike, not a signal of a repeatable machine. I've seen this pattern before — a burst of inbound at launch, founders count the month-over-month from a low base, and by month six the growth rate has collapsed to 3% and they're scrambling. Show me the acquisition channel breakdown and the cohort curves, and I'll tell you whether this is a machine or a fluke."
        },
        {
          "partner": "analyst",
          "position": "The operator is right to push on this. I'll add a number: $40K MRR annualizes to $480K ARR. At $333 ARPU and $1,200 CAC, the payback is 4.5 months — that's genuinely excellent, I won't dispute that. But the LTV computation that makes that CAC look attractive assumes a churn rate I have no evidence for. If this is an SMB business with 5% monthly churn — which is historically not uncommon in this segment — the cohort math deteriorates significantly. LTV/CAC drops from 7.4x to 4.4x, and more importantly, you're losing a significant fraction of your customer base every year. I need cohort data before I can accept the unit economics as a conviction point."
        },
        {
          "partner": "visionary",
          "position": "Reasonable pushback. The data gaps are real. But let me make a different point: the team composition actually gives me some comfort here. An ex-Stripe engineer and ex-Notion PM are not typical SMB founders. Stripe built its business on developer love and word-of-mouth in a market that was previously dominated by incumbent payment processors. Notion grew organically in a market full of established productivity tools. If this team is applying the same bottom-up, product-led playbook to field service, the 18% MoM growth might actually be a product-led signal, not a sales-driven one. That would be a structurally more durable engine."
        },
        {
          "partner": "operator",
          "position": "That is a compelling hypothesis and it would change my view if true. But it's a hypothesis. A product-led GTM for SMB field service has a different friction profile than product-led for developer tools — Notion's buyer was a knowledge worker who already lived in software. The field service buyer is a plumber or electrician who may be running the business from a phone and has real workflow switching costs. The PM from Notion would know that. I'd put 'evidence of product-led motion in a non-software-native buyer segment' at the top of the diligence list."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "topic": "Valuation — Is $16M Pre Defensible?",
      "exchanges": [
        {
          "partner": "analyst",
          "position": "At $20M post-money on $480K ARR, we're talking 41.7x trailing ARR. For context, the median seed-stage vertical SaaS deal in 2024-2025 closed at roughly 15-25x ARR. To justify 41.7x, you need either exceptional growth history — not four months — or a compelling forward multiple story with defensible growth assumptions. The 18% MoM rate projects to $3.5M ARR in 12 months, which would make this a ~5.7x forward ARR deal. That's reasonable, but only if the growth rate holds. We have four months of data. That's insufficient to anchor a forward projection with the confidence needed to justify paying a 70% premium to sector median."
        },
        {
          "partner": "visionary",
          "position": "I'd push back on the comparables framing. Median multiples are for median companies. A company growing 18% MoM with 80% gross margins and sub-5-month payback is not a median company. The valuation question is really: is this company on a trajectory that could return the fund? If they hit $3.5M ARR in 12 months and Series A investors are paying 15-20x forward ARR — which would be reasonable — that's a $52-70M post-money Series A valuation on our $20M entry. That's a 2.6-3.5x on paper in 12-18 months, which is fine for a seed check. The multiple question is about risk calibration, not absolute value."
        },
        {
          "partner": "operator",
          "position": "The visionary's math works if growth holds. And it might. But there's a hidden assumption in both your analyses: this math assumes we can do the deal without the portfolio conflict, without the missing data, and with the growth rate being real. Those are three non-trivial ifs stacked on each other. I don't have a strong view on valuation in isolation — at this growth rate, $16M pre is not unreasonable — but valuation is the last thing I would argue about. Let's solve the conflict question and the data room gaps first, and if those clear, the valuation is probably the easiest thing to negotiate."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "topic": "Team — Technical Strength vs. Commercial Gap",
      "exchanges": [
        {
          "partner": "operator",
          "position": "The team credentials are strong on the product and engineering side — nobody is questioning that. What concerns me operationally is what happens at $1M ARR. Founder-led sales for SMB can work up to a few hundred customers, but the ceiling is real. At some point you need a VP of Sales or Head of Revenue who knows the trades — who has relationships in the plumbing distributor network, who can get on stage at a PHCC conference, who knows how to build a channel through franchised service brands. Neither of these founders has that background. It's not disqualifying — plenty of technical founders have hired great GTM talent — but I want to hear the plan. Who is the first commercial hire, when, and why that profile?"
        },
        {
          "partner": "visionary",
          "position": "I would actually frame this as a risk and an opportunity. The risk is exactly what the operator described: scaling SMB field service requires either channel partnerships or a high-velocity outbound motion that the current team has not built. But the opportunity is that the ex-Stripe DNA might be signaling a different go-to-market entirely — software platforms, API integrations, distribution through vertical software partners or equipment manufacturers. If Cadence's strategy is to embed into the ServiceMax or QuickBooks ecosystem and capture customers through platform distribution rather than direct sales, then the lack of a traditional sales hire is a feature, not a bug."
        },
        {
          "partner": "analyst",
          "position": "I'll note for the record that the platform distribution hypothesis, while interesting, is speculative — it's not supported by any materials provided. What we have is two technical founders, 120 customers, and $40K MRR. The path they actually took to get here is unknown. I'd want to hear from the founders before assigning conviction to any GTM theory."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "topic": "Consensus and Path Forward",
      "exchanges": [
        {
          "partner": "analyst",
          "position": "My position is hard pass for this IC meeting, driven by the portfolio conflict. That said, I want to be constructive: if the conflict resolves — legal opinion, consent from ServiceBot and TechDispatch, a genuine assessment that Cadence is targeting a sufficiently differentiated market segment — I would be willing to revisit with a full data room. The underlying business, if the numbers check out, is interesting. But we cannot write a check into a direct competitor to two of our portfolio companies without that process."
        },
        {
          "partner": "operator",
          "position": "I'm with the analyst on the process. Hard pass today. Conditional interest if: one, the conflict question is formally resolved; two, founders provide channel acquisition breakdown and cohort data; three, I get reference calls with five customers. If those three gates clear, I'd be genuinely excited to continue the conversation."
        },
        {
          "partner": "visionary",
          "position": "Same conclusion, different emphasis. Hard pass today because we cannot responsibly proceed without conflict resolution. But I want to flag that if we pass, we should not ghost these founders. The combination of growth rate, team pedigree, and market timing is compelling enough that if we cannot do this deal, we should help them find the right firm — and we should resolve our conflict question promptly so we're not leaving a good company hanging."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "consensus_verdict": "hard_pass",
  "key_concerns": [
    "Blocking portfolio conflict: the fund holds ServiceBot (FSM) and TechDispatch (HVAC/Plumbing Scheduling SaaS), both direct overlaps with Cadence — investing requires formal legal opinion and portfolio company consent before any diligence can proceed",
    "GTM motion is opaque: no channel attribution for 120 customers acquired, no evidence of a repeatable acquisition engine beyond founder-led sales, unknown churn rate that underpins all unit economics calculations",
    "Valuation at ~41.7x trailing ARR is aggressive for a seed deal with four months of growth history and no cohort retention data to validate the forward projection",
    "Team composition gap: two technical co-founders with no GTM hire; no plan disclosed for first commercial hire in a relationship-driven SMB vertical",
    "Missing financial data: no burn rate, headcount, NDR/GRR, or cohort retention curves — the data room is incomplete for the investment decision required at this valuation"
  ],
  "diligence_requirements": [
    "Formal legal opinion and explicit written consent from ServiceBot and TechDispatch founders/LPs confirming the portfolio conflict is waivable",
    "Full customer acquisition breakdown by channel for all 120 customers, with sales cycle length and conversion data",
    "Monthly cohort retention table (GRR and NDR) covering all cohorts since launch — minimum 3 cohorts with 3+ months of history",
    "Current P&L, burn rate, headcount, and 24-month operating plan with Series A milestone targets",
    "Five customer reference calls, including at least one 3+ month tenure customer",
    "Articulation of the 10-year platform vision and differentiated wedge relative to ServiceBot and TechDispatch",
    "Valuation benchmarking memo with comparable seed transactions in vertical field-service SaaS (last 18 months)"
  ],
